		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4476&amp;p=33870#p33870"><![CDATA[
Following on from my experiments retro-fitting a GPS module inside my MD9600, I've now done an enhancement, by fitting a 3.5mm hack socket on the left side of the front panel.<br><br>I can then connect and external GPS puc by plugging it in.<br><br>The stereo jack socket I used is a PCB mount version, because it has 5 pins, and allows the internal GPS power and data to be disconnected when the external GPS is plugged in.<br><br>Warning.<br>I do not connect or disconnect the external GPS while the radio is turned on, as jack plugs can short their pins together while being plugged in or unplugged<br><div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="http://localhost/opengd77/download/file.php?id=2723&amp;sid=d2cf15b78aae742b32b607345078dd3b" class="postimage" alt="IMG_20241102_170306583.jpg" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div><div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="http://localhost/opengd77/download/file.php?id=2724&amp;sid=d2cf15b78aae742b32b607345078dd3b" class="postimage" alt="IMG_20241102_170319976_HDR.jpg" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div><div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="http://localhost/opengd77/download/file.php?id=2725&amp;sid=d2cf15b78aae742b32b607345078dd3b" class="postimage" alt="IMG_20241102_181351663.jpg" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div><br><br><br><br>I've now connected a M10 class GPS externally to the radio and it is receiving from 48 of a possible 45 satellites<br><div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="http://localhost/opengd77/download/file.php?id=2726&amp;sid=d2cf15b78aae742b32b607345078dd3b" class="postimage" alt="Untitled-1.jpg" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div><p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Sat Nov 02, 2024 7:23 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
		<entry>
		<author><name><![CDATA[VK3KYY]]></name></author>
		<updated>2024-10-18T06:26:55</updated>

		<published>2024-10-18T06:26:55</published>
		<id>http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4476&amp;p=33551#p33551</id>
		<link href="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4476&amp;p=33551#p33551"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: Retrofit GPS to a MD9600]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4476&amp;p=33551#p33551"><![CDATA[
Doing some further experiments, I ordered some u-blox M10 clone GPS modules from AliExpress hoping they would work better than the cheap ATGM336H module, becasue M10 class GPS can receive the 4 major GPS constellations, not just American and Chinese<br><br>However the data output from these is not currently compatible with the OpenGD77 firmware for a number of reasons.<br><br>1. These M10 class modules don't default to 9600, they often default to 38400<br>2. Some of NMEA sequences sent by u-blox M10 class devices seem to deviate from the normal NMEA standard and are not processed correctly by the firmware<br><br>Firsly.<br>Setting the module to 9600 baud before connecting to the MD9600 would partially solve the first problem, however the reason these modules default to 38400 baud is because they send much more data than modules which only handle 1 or 2 satellite constellations, and because they send all the data once per second, its likely it could not all be sent in one second<br><br>So.  I am writing support to detect if the GPS module uses a higher baud rate.<br><br>Secondly. T<br>o support the u-blox and also some other module types e.g Trimble. It will probably be necessary to modify the parsing of the GSV NMEA data messages. But because u-blox and Trimble and other other manufacturers do not seem to agree on the GSV messages, this may be difficult.<br><br><br>Overall, I am not actually seeing much benefit from using these M10 class modules, but so far I only tested 2 types, Quescan and EByte, and the EByte device I have is only the bare chip module not a whole PCB, so at the moment I don't have a backup battery for it, so I can't test the Hot Start<br><br><a href="https://www.aliexpress.com/item/1005005403914270.html" class="postlink">https://www.aliexpress.com/item/1005005403914270.html</a><br><br><a href="https://www.aliexpress.com/item/1005007789129084.html" class="postlink">https://www.aliexpress.com/item/1005007789129084.html</a><br><br>I made the mistake of not ordering the EByte device as a module on a PCB with an antenna, and it has been problematic to solder to this tiny device.<br>The reason I bought just the device not a module, is becasue I had the idea to fit it inside my UV380 or MD2017 instead of the official GPS, however it seems that these M10 class devices may need a larger antenna, becuase using a small antenna (10mm x 25mm) from a ATGM336H module, didn't work well, and the EByte module didn't receive many satellites.<br><br><br><br><br><br>So I've now ordered the same Ebyte device as a module with a separate antenna<br><br><a href="https://www.aliexpress.com/item/1005007740983988.html" class="postlink">https://www.aliexpress.com/item/1005007740983988.html</a><br><br>and also a "Beitian" brand GPS module on a PCB with backup battery  and antenna etc<br><br><a href="https://www.aliexpress.com/item/1005005209505077.html" class="postlink">https://www.aliexpress.com/item/1005005209505077.html</a><br><br><br><br><br>BTW.<br><br>I found this PC program from the manufacturer of the ATGM336H module, called GnssToolKit3 <br><a href="https://github.com/zxcwhale/GnssToolKit3-binaries/releases" class="postlink">https://github.com/zxcwhale/GnssToolKit ... s/releases</a><br><br>Which has a quite good display fo the satellite information.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Fri Oct 18, 2024 6:26 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
