		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4476&amp;p=33448#p33448"><![CDATA[
I thought I'd share some results of fitting a GPS module into a new MD9600 and and into an old MD9600<br><br>Both the radios didn't come with a GPS fitted.<br><br>I bought some ATGM336H modules from AliExpress, which are a separate receiver PCB and small rectangular antenna.<br>Like this module. <a href="https://www.aliexpress.com/item/4001147538089.html" class="postlink">https://www.aliexpress.com/item/4001147538089.html</a><br><div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="http://localhost/opengd77/download/file.php?id=2684&amp;sid=d2cf15b78aae742b32b607345078dd3b" class="postimage" alt="Screenshot 2024-10-12 at 15-20-49 GPS BDS dual-mode module flight control satellite positioning navigator ATGM336H replacement for arduino NEO-M8N NEO-6M - AliExpress.png" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div>The inside of the front panel of the MD9600 has some plastic lugs which are probably where the factor fitted GPS antenna is fitted, and the antennas with this sort of module fit inbetween these lug.<br><br>There seems to be some slight different in the placement of the lugs between the older e.g. V2 hardware radio and the V5 radios, so that some trimming may be needed on the lugs in the V2 radio so that the antenna will fit.<br><br>The antenna can then be glued in place.<br><br>The GPS module can easily be glued to the back of the PCB in the front panel of the radio, and connected to the pads on the PCB where the factory GPS module would be fitted.<br><br>This arrangement worked well on my V5 hardware radio, and I was able to receive up to 20 satellites even indoors.<br><br><br>However....<br><br>When I tried to fit the same module into my V2 hardware radio, it had great difficulty receiving more than 1 or 2 satellites.<br><br>Initially I thought I had a faulty antenna or faulty, GPS module, so I removed both and fitted another identical brand new module and brand new antenna, but the problem still persisted.<br><br>After a lot of experimentation, I found that if I power the GPS module from a separate supply that it worked OK, but not when I powered it from the radio.<br><br>I thought the problem may be a poor earth / negative connection to the display or poor supply voltage connection, as both go via a thin ribbon cable, however even using an additional earth wire etc didn't make any difference.<br><br><br>The problem was actually that the supply voltage to the GPS is very noisy on my V2 hardware radio.<br><br>I noticed on the schematic that the supply line is heavilty decoupled with multiple capacitors, but as far as I am aware, my V2 radio has all these decompiling capacitors and the only items not fitted are the GPS module and the antenna.<br><br>Just adding some decompiling didn't fix the problem, so finally I have built a L/C low pass filter consisting of a 1mH inductor and a 10uF Tant bead capacitor. These values probably aren't critical. I just happend these components in my box of spares, so I used what I had<br><br>But the V2 radio definitely needed the supply rail to be cleaned.<br><br>Possibly its only this type of module which can't handle a noisy supply rail, but other modules also have the same problem<br><br>I tried some GP-02 branded modules, but they had the same problem, and checking the NMEA data, I saw that both the GP-02 module and ATGM336H module both use the same GPS chip<br><br><br><br>I also noticed that this geration of GPS modules, only support American GPS, Chinese Beidou and also Japanese QNSS satellites. They don't support European Galileo or Russian GLONASS satellites or others like SBAS.<br><br>So as a matter of interest I have now ordered some M10 generation GPS modules, which should support all GPS satellite systems.<br>However I can't find any modules which have a small enough antenna, to make them very suitable to fit in the MD9600.<br><br>I have also ordered some bare modules, with no PCB, <a href="https://www.aliexpress.com/item/1005007789129084.html" class="postlink">https://www.aliexpress.com/item/1005007789129084.html</a>  in the hope that I can solder an antenna connection, however I don't know how practical this will be .<br><br>These new modules also default to higher baud rates which is not currently compatible with the OpenGD77 firmware, so I will have to reconfigure them before use or possibly modify the OpenGD77 firmware to detect the baud rate.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Sat Oct 12, 2024 4:55 am</p><hr />
